---
title: 'Recruiting Outreach Messages That Get Replies: 8 Templates for Tech Candidates'
publishedAt: 2026-11-11
type: companies
---
**Quick answer:** Recruiting messages that get replies are short (under 120 words), personal, and specific. Mention one real thing about the candidate's experience, say what the role is and why it fits them, include the salary range and work model, and end with a low-effort question like "Open to a 20-minute chat?". Send from a real person, ideally the hiring manager or founder, and follow up once after four to seven days. Generic, long or vague messages are ignored.

Good engineers receive many recruiting messages, and most look the same: "I came across your profile and was impressed by your background…". They delete them without reading. The messages that get replies show the sender actually looked at the person and has something worth their time.

## What makes a recruiting message work?

{% table %}
- Element
- Why it matters
---
- **A specific detail about them**
- Shows it isn't a mass message
---
- **What the role is, in one line**
- Lets them judge quickly
---
- **Why it fits them**
- Connects their experience to the job
---
- **Salary range and location**
- Answers the first two questions they'd ask
---
- **A real sender**
- Messages from hiring managers and founders are more credible
---
- **A small ask**
- "Open to a chat?" is easier to say yes to than "Apply here"
{% /table %}

## Template 1: The founder or hiring manager message

Best for: senior and founding roles.

> Hi Ananya,
>
> I'm Rahul, CTO at Fleetline. I read your post on cutting Postgres query times for your logistics dashboards, and it's exactly the problem we're working on: routing for 40,000 deliveries a day.
>
> We're hiring a Senior Backend Engineer (Go) to own that engine. Remote in India, ₹38–46 LPA plus ESOPs.
>
> Would you be open to a 20-minute chat next week?
>
> Rahul

## Template 2: The skills-match message

Best for: when the candidate's skills closely match the job.

> Hi Marco,
>
> Your work on the React Native app at Freshcart (offline sync and a 4.8 rating) stood out. We're building a field-service app with the same offline-first needs.
>
> The role: Mobile Engineer, React Native. Remote in Europe, €70–85k.
>
> Is this something you'd want to hear more about?

## Template 3: The career-move message

Best for: candidates whose profile says what they want next.

> Hi Priya,
>
> You mentioned you'd like to move from backend into platform engineering. Our Platform Engineer role would be that step: you'd own our Kubernetes setup and CI, with a senior SRE mentoring you for the first six months.
>
> Hybrid in Bengaluru (2 days a week), ₹30–36 LPA.
>
> Would a quick call be useful?

## Template 4: The past applicant message

Best for: strong candidates from earlier roles. See our guide to [candidate rediscovery](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/candidate-rediscovery-how-to-turn-old-resumes-and-spreadsheets-into-a-searchable-talent-pool).

> Hi Sam,
>
> You interviewed with us last spring for the frontend role, and the team loved your design system work. We went with someone more senior then, but we've now opened a role that fits you even better: Frontend Engineer on our new analytics product.
>
> Same team, remote, $110–130k. Interested in catching up?

## Template 5: The referral message

Best for: when someone recommended them.

> Hi Lena,
>
> Arjun Mehta (you worked together at Paybridge) suggested I reach out. He said you were the person everyone went to for tricky database problems.
>
> We're hiring a Staff Engineer for our data platform. Remote, $170–200k plus equity.
>
> Would you be open to a conversation?

## Template 6: The short follow-up

Send once, four to seven days after the first message.

> Hi Ananya, just bumping this in case it got buried. Happy to share more detail on the routing work if helpful, or no worries if the timing isn't right.

One follow-up is enough. A third message usually does more harm than good.

## Template 7: The "not now" reply

When someone says they're not looking:

> Thanks for letting me know, Ananya. Would you mind if I checked in again in six months? And if anyone in your network might be a fit, I'd appreciate an introduction.

## Template 8: The invite-to-apply message

Best for: candidates who look like a strong match and might apply directly.

> Hi Jo,
>
> We've just posted a Data Engineer role and your profile looks like a strong match: dbt, BigQuery and three years in e-commerce analytics. Remote in India, ₹25–32 LPA.
>
> If it looks interesting, you can apply in one click from this message. I'll read your application myself.

## Mistakes that kill reply rates

- **Long messages.** If it doesn't fit on a phone screen, it's too long.
- **Flattery without detail.** "Your impressive background" means you didn't read it.
- **Hiding the salary.** Many candidates won't reply without a range.
- **Wrong level or stack.** Messaging a staff engineer about a junior role signals carelessness.
- **Asking for a resume first.** You already have their profile.
- **Too many follow-ups.** One is fine. Three is spam.

## Consent-based outreach on GetHired

Cold outreach works better when candidates have already said they're open to it. On GetHired, applicants choose to make their profile public, and they can turn off outreach, block companies and report messages.

When you post a job, GetHired matches it against candidates' public profiles. You send a short message, optionally with the job attached, and the candidate replies **Interested** or **Not interested** in their Opportunities inbox. Their email, phone and links are shared only when they reply Interested or apply. Each company can contact a person at most three times in 90 days, and messages expire after 30 days, which keeps the inbox useful for candidates and replies higher for everyone. Read the full guide to [consent-based outreach](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-find-and-contact-candidates-who-want-to-be-found-consent-based-outreach).

You can also ask the [recruiter agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/meet-the-gethired-recruiter-agent-hire-tech-talent-from-one-chat) to draft personalised messages to your best matches. It shows you each message and waits for your approval before sending.

## Frequently asked questions

### How long should a recruiting message be?

Under 120 words. Three or four short paragraphs at most.

### Should recruiting messages include salary?

Yes. A range answers the candidate's first question and increases replies from people who are actually in your budget.

### When is the best time to send recruiting messages?

Weekday mornings in the candidate's time zone tend to work well, but personalisation matters far more than timing.

### How many follow-ups should I send?

One, after four to seven days. If there's no reply after that, move on.

### Should the founder or a recruiter send the message?

For senior and early-stage roles, messages from the founder or hiring manager are usually more credible. For high-volume roles, a recruiter who knows the role well is fine.

## Next steps

1. Rewrite your current outreach template using the elements above.
2. [Post your job on GetHired](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/auth/sign-up?company=true) to see matched candidates who are open to messages.
3. Track replies by job so you know which messages work.
